5, -0.0231170654296875, -0.0233306884765625, 0.00823974609375, 0.0012369155883789062, -0.0011196136474609375, 0.00913238525390625, 0.04437255859375, 0.033233642578125, 0.032989501953125, 0.01800537109375, 0.02239990234375, -0.00940704345703125, -0.0347900390625, -0.004306793212890625, 0.00406646728515625, 0.01413726806640625, -0.025177001953125, -0.01279449462890625, 0.0181732177734375, -0.00801849365234375, -0.036376953125, -0.0021877288818359375, -0.00911712646484375, 0.01093292236328125, -0.0159454345703125, -0.0108795166015625, -0.0185699462890625, -0.00901031494140625, 0.003345489501953125, -0.0218353271484375, 0.02783203125, -0.026458740234375, 0.036376953125, 0.0193634033203125, -0.047607421875, -0.018341064453125, 0.0018911361694335938, 0.027252197265625, -0.018768310546875, -0.0003285408020019531, 0.029510498046875, -0.01551055908203125, 0.0057525634765625, -0.019927978515625, -0.0052337646484375, 0.051025390625, -0.004974365234375, 0.025665283203125, 0.0022411346435546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA)\u2014also known as Clifford algebra in much of the literature\u2014to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits [1,2]. While the terms \"Clifford algebra\" and \"Geometric Algebra\" are sometimes used interchangeably, GA emphasizes the geometric interpretation and use of multivectors and the geometric product, whereas Clifford algebra is the underlying algebraic structure [1,2]. However, a key challenge remains: the ordinary differential equations that describe these systems must often be transformed from the real domain to the Laplace domain, and existing definitions of the Laplace transform do not readily extend to the framework of GA.\n\nIn particular, prior work has addressed extensions of the Laplace transform to certain hyper-complex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras [3,4]. These approaches often rely on properties such as commutativity of multiplication or the existence of a unique scalar exponential, which do not generally hold in GA due to the non-commutative geometric product and the presence of multivectors of different grades. For example, in GA, the exponential of a multivector does not always reduce to a simple scalar or vector form, complicating the direct extension of classical Laplace transform definitions.\n\nTo address this gap, we introduce a definition of the Laplace transform within the framework of Geometric Algebra. Our approach builds on and generalizes previous results for hyper-complex algebras, but is specifically tailored to multivector-valued functions and the geometric product structure unique to GA. In particular, we leverage the graded structure of GA and its ability to encode geometric transformations, which distinguishes it from other algebras. The proposed Laplace transform is defined by applying the transform component-wise to each grade of the multivector, while carefully handling the non-commutative multiplication in the algebra. This allows for a systematic treatment of multivector-valued signals and operators.\n\nWe focus on geometric algebras with signature less than or equal to 5. Here, the signature refers to the number of positive and negative squares in the quadratic form that defines the algebra (for example, the algebra of three-dimensional Euclidean space has signature (3,0)). This threshold is chosen because, for signatures above 5, the algebraic complexity and computational cost increase rapidly, making general analysis and practical computation significantly more challenging. Moreover, many physical systems of interest\u2014including those in robotics, electromagnetism, and classical mechanics\u2014can be effectively modeled within these lower signatures, making this restriction both practical and relevant rather than arbitrary.\n\nThe main contribution of this paper is the proposed definition of the Laplace transform in GA, along with a discussion of its key properties, including linearity and invertibility, and its applicability to the analysis of dynamical systems. We illustrate the use of our definition with examples drawn from electrical circuit analysis and rigid body dynamics modeled within GA. While our approach provides a systematic framework for Laplace analysis in GA, some limitations remain. For instance, the treatment of algebras with higher signatures or the development of efficient computational algorithms for large-scale systems are open questions for future work.\n\nThe remainder of the paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we review relevant background on Geometric Algebra, clarify terminology, and summarize related work on Laplace transforms in hyper-complex and non-commutative algebras. Section 3 presents our definition of the Laplace transform in GA and discusses its mathematical properties. Section 4 demonstrates its application to representative dynamical systems. Finally, Section 5 discusses potential implications for the analysis and control of complex systems in engineering and physics, as well as open questions and directions for further research.\n\nReferences:\n[1] D. Hestenes and G. Sobczyk, \"Clifford Algebra to Geometric Calculus,\" Reidel, 1984.\n[2] C. Doran and A. Lasenby, \"Geometric Algebra for Physicists,\" Cambridge University Press, 2003.\n[3] S. Sangwine and T. Ell, \"Hypercomplex Fourier Transforms of Color Images,\" IEEE Transactions on Image Processing, vol. 16, no. 1, pp. 22-35, 2007.\n[4] E. Hitzer, \"Quaternion and Clifford Fourier Transforms,\" in Advances in Applied Clifford Algebras, vol. 17, no. 3, pp. 497-517, 2007."}}